  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The task is to generate a sequence of non-negative heights for a step function that minimizes the evaluation function: 2*n*max(conv(sequence)) / (sum(sequence))^2. The previous program achieved an upper bound of 1.514818, and the goal is to improve this. I will use a hybrid approach:\n   - Start with a set of well-initialized sequences from different families (uniform, exponential, two-level, quadratic bump).\n   - Evaluate each sequence using the actual function.\n   - Perform a series of gradient-based searches to refine the sequences.\n   - Use decomposition and parallelization to maintain feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - The original problem: Minimize (2*n*max(conv(s,s))) / (sum(s))^2 for a sequence s \u2208 \u211d\u208a^(n)\n   - Let M = max(conv(s,s)). The problem can be rewritten as:\n        min M / ( (sum(s))^4 / (2*n)^2 )   [since (2*n)/(sum(s))^2 is fixed, minimizing the ratio is equivalent to minimizing M/(sum(s))^2]\n   - We will use the gradient of the squared coefficients and the maximum convolution value.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a. Initialization:\n      i. Generate candidate sequences of length 2048 (the longest) from several families.\n      ii. Families to choose from: Uniform, Exponential, Two-level, Quadratic bump (in both directions).\n      iii. Use the provided `height_sequence_1` as a starting candidate if available.\n    b. Search process:\n      i. For each candidate sequence, perform a gradient descent-like optimization.\n      ii. Compute the gradient by finite differences around the critical points (convolution maxima).\n      iii. Adjust the sequence to decrease both the convolution max and the sum.\n      iv. Use a restart strategy if the optimization stalls.\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Main search: Gradient-based descent with finite differences.\n   - Initialization sets: Several families and also random small variations.\n   - Parameter tuning: Cooldown time for gradient steps to allow stabilization.\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Evaluation time dominated by FFT convolution calls (approx O(n log n)).\n   - We will run evaluations carefully, with timeout checks during the gradient descent.\n   - Budget splits: 30% for initialization, 70% for refinements.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Each candidate sequence must be nonnegative and have a finite sum \u2265 0.01.\n   - Use the actual `evaluate_sequence` function inside the gradient evaluation.\n   - Return the sequence with the smallest evaluated objective.",
